  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq1Question" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What is a Satoshi (sat)?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq1Answer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A Satoshi, or “sat”, is the smallest unit of Bitcoin. One Satoshi equals 0.00000001 BTC, and 100,000,000 Satoshis make up a single Bitcoin. It is named after Bitcoin's pseudonymous creator, Satoshi Nakamoto.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq2Question" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How many Satoshis are in one Bitcoin?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq2Answer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>There are exactly 100,000,000 (one hundred million) Satoshis in one Bitcoin (BTC).</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq3Question" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>How do I convert Satoshis to my local currency?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq3Answer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Enter an amount in the converter to see its value in the selected currency, or convert your currency to sats. Choose a currency in the converter or the table. Use the top bar to change the site language.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq4Question" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Why do Bitcoin prices differ slightly between exchanges?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq4Answer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Each exchange has its own order book and liquidity, so prices vary over time. Freedom Blaze combines available rates from Blockchain.info, Bitstamp, CoinGecko, Coinbase, Gemini and Coingate to calculate an average price.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq5Question" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Is the Freedom Blaze Sats converter free to use?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="SatsInfo.Faq5Answer" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Yes. The converter, live exchange rates and Bitcoin news are completely free, with no sign-up or account required.</value>
  </data>
